\chapter{Registering and contributing a kernel}
\label{ch:115}
\index{Registering and contributing a kernel}

Start a kernel contribution with a declared representation, supported target, limits, and one documented implementation placement. Add a C example and a test that executes the advertised API. Record the header, manual, dependencies and implementation kind in the shared inventory workflow. The installed pre-commit hook described by the repository can refresh the generated registrations from staged inputs.

\section{Prove the advertised profile}
Run a strict C11 caller with the intended dependency closure. Inspect linkage from a separate consumer and test failure after partial initialization. Check the manual's limit values against the actual source. If the implementation is static inline, keep that category in the manifest and supply evidence before promising an external wrapper. If On1x is meant to call the kernel, open a separate review for the exact target, contract and admission model. Registration, C execution and safe On1x execution are separately demonstrable milestones.

\section{Detect a header that every generated list forgot}
Imagine submitting a new static-inline header and forgetting the
registration update. Comparing an archive catalogue to a discovery
catalogue could show perfect agreement: neither knows the new header
exists. The registration regression therefore starts outside both
catalogues. It enumerates canonical header paths on disk, accounts
separately for reserved headers, and compares the remaining set with
the registered module headers. A count alone would not catch a
missing path replaced by an unrelated extra path. The test checks
both the set and the number of records, so registering one header
under two module names also fails.

Next follow the test's QVM case. Its discovery closure includes
container-core, hash inputs for both headers, and no implementation
switch for either one. When a switched consumer needs those internal
definitions, they may appear as dependencies in its plan. Requesting
QVM explicitly as another top-level archive module is different: the
planner rejects its discovery-only profile even if the consumer already
requires the header. Granting the explicit request on the strength of
the dependency would advertise an external ABI which the dependency
did not gain.

Finally check the path from a registered name back to a source request.
The regression constructs batches of distinct aliases to stay below
the per-program import budget and compares the reported module names
with the requested names in order. This detects a normalization
collision without claiming to have linked or executed a program
containing every kernel.

